-- #1111 A-2.7 REGRESSION GUARD: the engine's `T(..)` index must not bind a
-- constructor the FRONT END never puts in scope.
--
-- 🚨 THIS FIXTURE EXISTS BECAUSE A-2.7'S FIRST CUT GOT IT WRONG, and the way it got
-- it wrong is the transferable part.  That cut built the index arm-for-arm against
-- `eval.collectCtors` — which is deliberately UNFILTERED, because every constructor
-- a module declares needs a cell whether or not anyone outside can name it.  That
-- makes it the right peer for "does this cell exist" and the WRONG peer for "may an
-- import bind this name".  The second is a SCOPE question.
--
-- The distinction bites because the import frame OUT-RANKS `globalCells`: an
-- over-indexed entry does not merely add a name, it SHADOWS the one that was
-- correctly reached before.  With an unconditional `DNewtype` arm, `NT(..)` bound
-- nmodv's `Wrap` and shadowed the `Wrap` the front end had actually resolved.
--
-- Expected output, derived by hand BEFORE any capture and independently of any
-- engine: nmodv exports no constructor, so `NT(..)` puts nothing in scope and the
-- only `Wrap` the front end can bind is kmodv's arity-2 one — `unKT (Wrap 3 4)` =
-- `4 - 3` = `1`.  Confirmed independently by the typechecker, which reports
-- `w = Wrap 3` as `w : Int -> KT` in this module.  Then `unNT (mkNT 9)` = `9`,
-- reached through nmodv's own exported functions, which is the ONLY way its `Wrap`
-- is reachable at all.  So `1` then `9`.
--
-- ⚠️ COUNTERFACTUAL — a regression guard's counterfactual is the BROKEN CUT, not
-- pristine `main`.  Measured on THIS FIXTURE (not on an equivalent probe), through
-- BOTH gates that read this directory, on three binaries cold-bootstrapped in one
-- worktree:
--   * pristine `main` `ae39ec41`: `ok` on both — the shape was already correct, so
--     this really is a guard and not a drain;
--   * A-2.7's first cut (unconditional `DNewtype` arm): `FAIL` on both;
--   * gating that arm on `newtypePub`: `FAIL` on both, unchanged — `export newtype`
--     SETS `newtypePub`, so the obvious gate is a no-op here.  The arm had to be
--     REMOVED, not filtered.  Recorded because the plausible fix is the wrong one
--     and only running it says so.
--
-- ⚠️ WHY THIS IS NOT IN `test/llvm_fixtures_modules/` LIKE ITS SIBLINGS.  That corpus
-- runs all three engines, and the NATIVE arm is wrong on this shape on `main`:
-- `private_mangle`'s ctor-export index DOES have a `DNewtype` arm, so the emitter
-- binds nmodv's `Wrap` and the built binary dies `E-NONEXHAUSTIVE-MATCH` while
-- `run` prints the correct `1`.  That is a pre-existing run≠build divergence, filed
-- separately and pinned in `test/must_fail_fixtures/`; enrolling this shape in a
-- native gate would pin someone else's open bug as this unit's expectation.  Here
-- it grades the two INTERPRETER drivers, which is where A-2.7's index lives.
--
-- ⚠️ THE CORPUS HAD NO `newtype` AT ALL.  Before this fixture there were zero
-- `newtype` declarations in `test/eval_modules_fixtures/`,
-- `test/llvm_fixtures_modules/` or `test/eval_typed_modules_fixtures/`, and zero in
-- `compiler/`, `stdlib/` and `sqlite/` — so the compiler cannot self-host into the
-- shape either, and no gate in the tree could fail on it.  Abstract `export data` is
-- not a substitute: resolve refuses `T(..)` on it LOUDLY (*"exports no constructors
-- … exported abstractly"*) before any engine is reached, so the newtype is the only
-- spelling that reaches this index at all.
import nmodv.{NT(..), mkNT, unNT}
import kmodv.{KT, unKT}

main =
  println (unKT (Wrap 3 4))
  println (unNT (mkNT 9))
